AI News Chatbot Agent: Code and Installation Guide
Overview
This chatbot will be a Python script that runs in your terminal (command prompt). It uses the requests library to connect to the NewsAPI, which offers a free plan suitable for a personal project like this.
Deliverable 1: Source Code and Files
You'll need two files:
news_chatbot.py: The main Python script.
requirements.txt: A file listing the necessary Python libraries.
File 1: news_chatbot.py
This script provides a simple command-line interface (CLI) to get U.S. and World news headlines.
Python
# news_chatbot.py

import requests
import json
import os

# --- Configuration ---
# You need to get a free API key from NewsAPI (https://newsapi.org/)
# and replace 'YOUR_NEWS_API_KEY' below.
# It's best practice to use an environment variable, but for simplicity,
# we'll place it here.
API_KEY = "YOUR_NEWS_API_KEY"
BASE_URL = "https://newsapi.org/v2/top-headlines"

def fetch_headlines(country_code, category):
    """Fetches top headlines for a given country and category."""
    if API_KEY == "YOUR_NEWS_API_KEY":
        print("ERROR: Please replace 'YOUR_NEWS_API_KEY' in the script with your actual NewsAPI key.")
        return None

    params = {
        'country': country_code,
        'category': category,
        'apiKey': API_KEY,
        'pageSize': 5  # Limit to 5 headlines for conciseness
    }

    try:
        response = requests.get(BASE_URL, params=params)
        response.raise_for_status()  # Raise an exception for bad status codes (4xx or 5xx)
        data = response.json()
        return data['articles']
    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:
        print(f"An error occurred while fetching news: {e}")
        print("Please check your internet connection or your API key.")
        return None

def display_headlines(articles, region, category):
    """Prints the fetched articles to the console."""
    print(f"\n--- Top 5 {category.capitalize()} Headlines in {region.upper()} ---")
    if not articles:
        print("No articles found.")
        return

    for i, article in enumerate(articles, 1):
        title = article.get('title', 'No Title')
        source = article.get('source', {}).get('name', 'Unknown Source')
        url = article.get('url', 'No URL')

        print(f"{i}. {title}")
        print(f"   Source: {source}")
        print(f"   URL: {url}")
        print("-" * 20)

def main_chatbot_loop():
    """The main interactive loop for the chatbot."""
    print("\n👋 Welcome to the AI News Chatbot Agent!")
    print("You are running version 1.0.0.")
    print("Available Commands:")
    print("  'us news' - Get top U.S. headlines (General)")
    print("  'world news' - Get top World headlines (General)")
    print("  'us tech' - Get top U.S. Technology headlines")
    print("  'world business' - Get top World Business headlines")
    print("  'categories' - See all available categories")
    print("  'exit' or 'quit' - Close the chatbot")
    print("-" * 40)

    while True:
        user_input = input("Enter your news request (e.g., 'us news'): ").strip().lower()

        if user_input in ['exit', 'quit']:
            print("Thank you for using the News Chatbot. Goodbye! 👋")
            break
        elif user_input == 'categories':
            print("\nAvailable Categories (can be appended to 'us' or 'world' requests):")
            print("  business, entertainment, general, health, science, sports, technology")
            continue
        elif user_input == 'us news':
            articles = fetch_headlines(country_code='us', category='general')
            display_headlines(articles, 'US', 'General')
        elif user_input == 'world news':
            # NewsAPI uses 'country' codes. 'gb' (UK) is a good proxy for major English-language World News.
            articles = fetch_headlines(country_code='gb', category='general')
            display_headlines(articles, 'WORLD (UK Proxy)', 'General')
        elif user_input == 'us tech':
            articles = fetch_headlines(country_code='us', category='technology')
            display_headlines(articles, 'US', 'Technology')
        elif user_input == 'world business':
            articles = fetch_headlines(country_code='gb', category='business')
            display_headlines(articles, 'WORLD (UK Proxy)', 'Business')
        else:
            print("I don't recognize that command. Please try 'us news', 'world news', or 'categories'.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main_chatbot_loop()

File 2: requirements.txt
This file ensures you install the correct Python packages.
# requirements.txt
requests


Deliverable 2: Step-by-Step Installation Instructions
Create a new directory (folder) for your project and save the two files above inside it.
Document File: Chatbot Installation Guide (MacBook Pro)
Prerequisites: Your MacBook Pro comes with Python pre-installed, but it's best to use a more modern version. We will assume you have a working terminal and internet connection.
Step 1: Get Your API Key 🔑
Go to the NewsAPI website: https://newsapi.org/
Click "Get API Key" and sign up for a free account.
Once signed up, your unique API key will be displayed. Copy this key.
Step 2: Set Up the Project Directory
Open your Terminal application (Press Command + Space and search for "Terminal").
Create a project directory and navigate into it:
Bash
mkdir ai-news-chatbot
cd ai-news-chatbot


Using a text editor (like Nano or VS Code), create the two files:
Create requirements.txt:
Bash
nano requirements.txt
# Paste the content for requirements.txt (just 'requests') and save (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter)


Create news_chatbot.py:
Bash
nano news_chatbot.py
# Paste the Python code for news_chatbot.py
# IMPORTANT: Find the line `API_KEY = "YOUR_NEWS_API_KEY"` and replace it with your actual key:
# e.g., API_KEY = "3a8b4c5d6e7f..."
# Save the file (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter)


Step 3: Install Dependencies
It is a best practice to use a virtual environment to keep project dependencies separate. Create and activate one:
Bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
(You should now see (venv) at the start of your terminal prompt.)
Install the necessary libraries using the requirements.txt file:
Bash
pip install -r requirements.txt


Step 4: Run the Chatbot! 🚀
Execute the Python script:
Bash
python3 news_chatbot.py


The chatbot prompt will appear! Enter commands like us news, world business, or exit.

Deliverable 3: Instructions for Uploading to GitHub
This guide assumes you have a GitHub account and the Git version control system installed on your MacBook Pro (it's usually pre-installed).
Step 1: Initialize Git and Create a Repository
Make sure you are in your project folder (ai-news-chatbot):
Bash
cd ai-news-chatbot


Initialize a local Git repository:
Bash
git init


Create a .gitignore file to avoid uploading unnecessary files like the virtual environment:
Bash
nano .gitignore
# Paste the following and save:
# venv/


Step 2: Commit Your Files
Add all the project files to the staging area:
Bash
git add .


Commit the files with an initial message:
Bash
git commit -m "Initial commit of the AI News Chatbot"


Step 3: Create GitHub Repository and Push
Go to your GitHub account in your web browser.
Click the '+' icon in the top right and select "New repository."
Name it something like ai-news-chatbot, choose Public or Private, and click "Create repository."
On the next screen, GitHub will show you commands to link your local repository. Use the following two lines (replacing the URL with the one GitHub provides):
Bash
# 1. Link your local repository to the remote one on GitHub
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/ai-news-chatbot.git

# 2. Push your committed files to the main branch
git push -u origin main


Your code is now on GitHub! You can confirm by refreshing your repository page.

